WEBVTT
TITLE Resolution Refutation And Horn Clauses — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
A clause is a disjunction of literals; a set of clauses is a conjunction of them.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
Resolution does not build a proof of the goal. It refutes the goal together with the knowledge base.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
Add the negation of the goal to the clauses. If the whole set is unsatisfiable, the goal follows.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
So the whole method asks one question: can all these clauses be true at the same time?

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
Five steps take any formula to clauses: implications out, negations in, distribute, split the conjunction, name the clauses.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
P implies Q and R becomes two clauses: not P or Q, and not P or R.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
Negating the goal is part of the method: not (P implies Q) becomes P and not Q, which is two unit clauses.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
The empty clause is falsity, and the empty set of clauses is trivially true.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
Two clauses that disagree on exactly one variable produce a new clause.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
Cancel the pair, keep everything else, drop repeated literals. That is the resolvent.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
The pivot must be positive in one parent and negative in the other. Agreement on a variable gives nothing.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
A resolvent containing a variable and its negation is a tautology, and it is thrown away.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
The rule is sound: every assignment that satisfies both parents satisfies the resolvent.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
There are two cases and no third: the pivot is true, or the pivot is false.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
If the pivot is true, the clause holding its negation must be satisfied by something else.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
Adding a resolvent never changes satisfiability, so a satisfiable set can never produce the empty clause.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
Four clauses over two variables: P or Q, P or not Q, not P or Q, not P or not Q.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
The first pair differs only on Q, so resolve on Q and get P.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
The second pair differs only on Q as well, so resolve on Q and get not P.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
P and not P give the empty clause. The set is unsatisfiable, and every original clause is used once.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
Resolution is complete for refutation: an unsatisfiable set always has a refutation.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
It is not complete for derivation: from the single clause P you can derive nothing at all.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
That is not a defect. To show P entails Q, you refute P together with not Q, and that set is satisfiable.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
Refutation answers one question only: can this set of clauses be satisfied?

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
A Horn clause has at most one positive literal, so at most one conclusion.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
Read as a rule: not A or not B or C says A and B together give C.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
Unit propagation marks a fact, then fires every rule whose premises are all marked.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
On Horn clauses this settles satisfiability in linear time.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
Five Horn clauses over five atoms: two facts, two rules and one goal.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
The goal is never triggered, because E is never derived, so the set is satisfiable and the marked atoms form its smallest model.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
Now the four clauses over P and Q. There is no unit clause, so propagation cannot start.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
Yet the set is unsatisfiable, and resolution refutes it in three steps. Unit propagation alone is not complete.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
Resolving on two variables at once is unsound: P or Q with not P or not Q.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
Cancelling both pairs gives the empty clause, but P true and Q false satisfies both parents.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
Done legally, both resolvents are tautologies and are dropped, so nothing new is derived.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
Complete does not mean cheap: the pigeonhole clauses grow from 6 to 73 to more than 900.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
Step one: put the negation of the goal into clauses, and add those clauses to the set.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
Step two: find a complementary pair, resolve on that one variable, and keep the result if it is new and not a tautology.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
Step three: repeat until the empty clause appears, or until no new clause can be made.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
Five traps, one defence: one pivot, one pair, and the empty clause as the only verdict.

